## Decoding the question
The stem gives a fact — **every province and Islamabad now has a domestic-violence law** — and asks three things. "**Critically evaluate**" the legal framework: its **strengths and weaknesses**. "**Why has legislation alone failed**" asks for **causes**, best organised by level (society, institutions, state). "**Workable strategies**" means **realistic** measures, not slogans. Name each law with its year, and use dated data (PDHS 2017–18; SSDO 2024).

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Domestic violence** is physical, sexual, psychological or economic abuse by a person with whom a woman lives or has lived in a household — a husband, in-law, father or brother. The UN's **Declaration on the Elimination of Violence against Women** (==1993==) names the **family** as the first site of violence.

#### ii) The argument
Between ==2013== and ==2025== Pakistan filled a long legal gap: Sindh, Balochistan, Punjab, Khyber Pakhtunkhwa and the Islamabad Capital Territory all enacted domestic-violence laws. The laws are **progressive in design** but have had **limited effect**, because violence is rooted in **patriarchal norms**, sustained by **weak enforcement** and **impunity**, and met by a state with **little capacity**. Laws are necessary, but only a **five-P strategy** can turn them into protection.

### 2. The scale of the problem
The **Pakistan Demographic and Health Survey 2017–18** found that **28%** of ever-married women had experienced physical violence since age 15, and **34%** had experienced spousal physical, sexual or emotional violence. Most suffer in silence: **56%** never sought help. The **Sustainable Social Development Organization** recorded **32,617** cases of gender-based violence in ==2024==, with conviction rates of about **0.5%** for rape and honour killing. The gap between the scale of abuse and the response of the law is the core of the problem.

### 3. The legal framework
The **Constitution** guarantees security of person (**Article 9**), dignity (**Article 14**), equality and special measures for women (**Article 25**) and protection of the family (**Article 35**). Pakistan acceded to **CEDAW** in ==1996==; its General Recommendations **19** (==1992==) and **35** (==2017==) treat gender-based violence as **discrimination**. Pakistan has also committed to **SDG target 5.2** — eliminating all violence against women and girls — and its **GSP+** trade status (==2014==) depends on implementing human-rights conventions.

### 4. The five laws
After the **18th Amendment** (==2010==) made the subject provincial, each legislature acted separately:

::: fig timeline | Pakistan's domestic-violence laws, 2013–2025.
@layout vertical
2013: Sindh Domestic Violence (Prevention and Protection) Act — the first
2014: Balochistan Domestic Violence (Prevention and Protection) Act
2016: Punjab Protection of Women against Violence Act (24 February)
2021: Khyber Pakhtunkhwa Domestic Violence against Women Act — after years of opposition
2025: Islamabad Domestic Violence (Prevention and Protection) Act — NA 13 November, Senate 28 November
:::

The **Punjab Act 2016** is the most detailed. Its section 2(r) recognises **domestic violence, sexual violence, psychological abuse, economic abuse, stalking and cybercrime**; complaints go to the **Family Court**, which must decide within **90 days**; and it provides **interim, protection, residence and monetary orders**, including a **GPS bracelet** for grave violence.

### 5. Strengths of the framework

#### i) Wider definitions
The laws recognise that violence is not only beating: **economic abuse** (denying food, clothing or a woman's own income) and **psychological abuse** are now named in law.

#### ii) Civil remedies
**Protection and residence orders** let a woman stay safely in her home rather than lose it — a practical relief that criminal law alone never offered.

#### iii) Institutions
The Punjab Act created **District Women Protection Committees**, **Women Protection Officers** and one-stop **Violence against Women Centres** — the first opened in **Multan** in ==2017== — bringing police, doctors, prosecutors and counsellors under one roof.

### 6. Weaknesses in the laws themselves
The laws differ from province to province in definitions and penalties, so a woman's protection depends on where she lives. Several rely on **mediation and reconciliation**, which can push women back into abusive homes. Penalties for breach are light — up to **one year** for a first breach in Punjab. And the laws met organised resistance: the **Council of Islamic Ideology** and religious parties called the Punjab Act a threat to the family, delaying its rules and centres, while the Khyber Pakhtunkhwa law took years to pass.

### 7. Why legislation fails: society and norms
Violence persists because the norms behind it are untouched. Many men — and many women — still believe a husband may "**discipline**" his wife; the PDHS found wide acceptance of wife-beating for at least one reason. Ideas of ***izzat*** and ***ghairat*** make a complaint a **family dishonour**. Women's **economic dependence** — female labour-force participation is only **24.4%** (LFS ==2024–25==) — and denial of inheritance leave them nowhere to go. **Social learning theory** (Bandura) explains how violence passes from parents to children.

### 8. Why legislation fails: police, courts and impunity
Institutions designed to enforce the laws often defeat them. Police **refuse or delay FIRs**, urge "compromise" and lack women officers. Investigation and forensics are weak. Courts are slow, witnesses turn hostile and families pressure women to withdraw. The result is a conviction rate of about **0.5%** — and **impunity**, which teaches abusers that violence is costless.

::: fig cycle | Why domestic-violence laws have not stopped violence.
@centre Laws on paper, violence in practice
Norms excuse "discipline" and honour
Women do not know their rights
Police delay FIRs; push compromise
Slow courts; hostile witnesses
About 0.5% convictions
Impunity encourages repeat abuse
:::

### 9. Why legislation fails: the state's capacity
Laws were passed without the **money and staff** to implement them. One-stop centres exist in few districts; ***Dar-ul-Aman*** shelters are scarce and often prison-like; helplines are unknown to rural women. There is no national database on domestic violence, and provincial capacity varies widely after the 18th Amendment. **Lori Heise**'s ecological model (==1998==) explains the overall failure: the laws act mainly at the **society** level, while violence is produced at the **individual, relationship and community** levels too.

### 10. Workable strategies: prevention and protection

#### i) Prevention
Teach **gender equality and consent** in schools and keep girls in school; work with **men and boys**, as the Islamabad NGO **Rozan** does; enlist religious scholars and Friday sermons to reject violence as un-Islamic; support women's **economic independence** through jobs, inheritance enforcement and the **Benazir Income Support Programme**; and enforce the **18-year marriage age** and marriage registration.

#### ii) Protection
Make **protection orders** quick and enforced; open a one-stop **centre in every district**; build dignified, well-run shelters; publicise **24-hour helplines** such as the **Punjab Women Helpline 1043** and the federal **1099**; and train **Lady Health Workers** as first responders in villages.

### 11. Workable strategies: prosecution, provision and partnership

#### i) Prosecution
Make **FIR registration compulsory**, with penalties for police who refuse; recruit more **women police**; build forensic capacity; and assign domestic-violence cases to **special courts** with fast, in-camera trials, as the **Anti-Rape Act 2021** did for sexual offences.

#### ii) Provision
Offer free **legal aid, medical care, counselling** and **vocational training**, in local languages, with outreach to rural, minority and disabled women.

#### iii) Partnership
Build a **co-ordinated community response**, on the **Duluth** model, linking police, courts, health, shelters and NGOs such as the **Aurat Foundation** and **Shirkat Gah**; harmonise the five laws through the **Council of Common Interests**; and create a national **GBV database**.

### 12. Conclusion
Pakistan now has a domestic-violence law in every province and in Islamabad — a real achievement of the women's movement. Yet laws on paper have not become protection in practice, because norms, police and courts, and state capacity lag behind. The workable path combines **quick wins** — compulsory FIRs, enforced protection orders, centres and helplines in every district — with **long-term prevention** that changes norms of masculinity and honour. Only then will the promise of Articles 9, 14 and 25 reach the women behind closed doors.

## Why this answer scores
- Uses the stem's fact: names **all five laws** with their years and describes the **Punjab Act 2016** in detail.
- Evaluates **critically**: strengths and weaknesses of the laws themselves come before the causes of failure.
- Organises the causes **by level** (norms, institutions, state capacity) and ties them to **Heise's ecological model**.
- Strategies are **workable** and organised by the **five Ps**, with Pakistani institutions named.
- A **timeline** and a **cycle** figure carry the argument; data are dated (PDHS 2017–18, SSDO 2024, LFS 2024–25).
